For short, single-statement work where failure means 'just run it again', plain JDBC keeps the code surface tiny.

**Signs plain JDBC is enough**
- The work is a single SQL statement (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) repeated N times
- Total runtime is under a few minutes
- Failure handling = re-launch from scratch, no harm done
- No need for skip/retry beyond a simple try/catch
- No audit requirement beyond your application logs

**Idiomatic plain JDBC batch with `JdbcTemplate.batchUpdate`**

```java
@Component
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class OrderArchiver {
    private final JdbcTemplate jdbc;

    public int archive(List<Order> orders) {
        String sql = "INSERT INTO orders_archive (id, customer_id, amount) VALUES (?, ?, ?)";
        int[] counts = jdbc.batchUpdate(sql, new BatchPreparedStatementSetter() {
            public void setValues(PreparedStatement ps, int i) throws SQLException {
                Order o = orders.get(i);
                ps.setLong(1, o.getId());
                ps.setLong(2, o.getCustomerId());
                ps.setBigDecimal(3, o.getAmount());
            }
            public int getBatchSize() { return orders.size(); }
        });
        return Arrays.stream(counts).sum();
    }
}
```

**Performance settings that actually matter**
- `rewriteBatchedStatements=true` on MySQL — collapses multiple INSERTs into a single multi-VALUES statement; 5-10x faster
- `reWriteBatchedInserts=true` on PostgreSQL — similar batching optimization
- Batch size 100-1000 rows per `executeBatch()` — the same tuning logic as Spring Batch chunk size
- Disable autocommit and commit explicitly per batch

**When to graduate to Spring Batch**
- The job grew past 30 minutes and a midnight failure now means restart costs hours
- You're hand-rolling skip-bad-row logic with counters and dead-letter files
- You need an audit log of every run for compliance (SOX, GDPR, financial reporting)
- The job needs to compose multiple discrete steps with conditional flow
